    "recommendations": "• Install a clearly visible address marker at the driveway entrance so responding units can confirm the correct property from the road.\n• Mark the driveway entrance with reflective posts on both sides to help responders locate and align with the access point in low-light or smoky conditions.\n• Post a property map or structure diagram near the driveway entrance showing the location of all structures, the water feature, and the equipment storage area.\n• Keep the access drive clear of parked vehicles, equipment, and overhanging branches for its full length to allow emergency vehicles to reach all structures without obstruction.\n• Identify and mark a dedicated turnaround area near the structures so emergency vehicles are not forced to back out the full length of the driveway.\n• Establish a secondary foot access path to the rear of the property so responders are not solely dependent on the main driveway to reach all structures.\n• Mark the water feature with visible boundary indicators so responders moving across the open terrain at night or in reduced visibility do not approach the edge unexpectedly.\n• Keep a clear path between the main residence and both detached structures so responders can move between buildings without navigating around parked equipment or landscaping.\n• Keep the equipment storage area organized so responders can quickly identify and avoid stored hazards during operations.\n• If the perimeter fencing includes a rear or side gate, keep it unlocked"
  },
